body:
Found while programming a simple 2.5D end-cap plate (user's project) with the CamCraft **v0.19.0** release CLI (macOS arm64), driven headlessly over MCP. Re-checked on upstream main **d00243a5**: **still reproduces**.

## What happens

A Bore op whose hole list was edited (`edit_operation` with `holes`, after `detect_holes`) is generated and reads `ok`. Save the project, open it in a fresh session: the op reads **`stale`**. The user's first delivered `endcap.camproj` had its holes op stale on reopen; it had to be regenerated, saved and reopened again.

**Which field changes.** The edited floor was typed as `0.190 in - 0.040 in` = `3.8099999999999996` mm. The file stores it as `3.81`: `HoleDto::from_hole` (`crates/app/src/project/params.rs`) snaps every hole coordinate onto the file's lattice (`lattice(h.floor_z)`). The op's params and its stored `stamp` are identical in the saved and re-saved files, but the stamp was computed in memory from the unsnapped `3.8099999999999996`; the reloaded op's params now hash differently, so it reads stale. With the detected values unedited (already on the lattice) the op reopens `ok`.

Same class as #1351 (the lattice snap changed a predecessor's `path_sig`) and #169 (float round-trip). The snap at save is fine; the typed value just never went through it at the command.

## Minimal repro (MCP)

```
set_units      {"unit": "inch"}
import_model   {"path": "End Caps-End capSupport Plate.step", "name": "part"}
move_model     {"name": "part", "position": [-37.9, 0, 4.826], "rotation": [0, 0, 0.7071067811865476, 0.7071067811865476]}
add_setup      {"name": "Setup 1"}
set_stock      {"box": {"dx": 184.15, "dy": 107.95, "dz": 15.334}}
set_wcs        {"stock_point": {"x": "center", "y": "center", "z": "max"}}
add_tool       {"catalog": "EM-0400-2FL", "name": "EM4"}
add_operation  {"kind": "Bore", "tool": "EM4", "name": "holes", "source": "part", "params": {"stepdown": 2.0}}
detect_holes   {"name": "holes"}
edit_operation {"name": "holes", "params": {"holes": [the four Ø6 holes from get_job, each with "floor_z": 3.8099999999999996, "through": false]}}
generate {} ; get_job -> holes "ok"
save_project {"path": "p.camproj"}
-- new session --
open_project {"path": "p.camproj"} ; get_job -> holes "stale"
```

Without the `edit_operation` step, the op reopens `ok`.

## Expected

A project saved with every op `ok` reopens with every op `ok` (the #169 law), whatever float the user typed.

## Done means

- Typed hole values (and any other field the file snaps to its lattice) are snapped when the command is created, so the in-memory value, the stamp and the file agree; or the stamp hashes the snapped value.
- A pinning test: edit a hole floor to an off-lattice value, generate, save, load into a fresh world, and the op reads `ok`.
- A sweep of the other DTOs that call `lattice(...)` on save for the same gap.

Reported by the maintainer (2026-11-13, in chat, v0.19.0-era build): "stepover value doesnt seem to be respected. Going fro 3mm to 25mm stepover doesnt change the tool path one bit."

What happens: changing an operation's stepover from 3 mm to 25 mm leaves the generated toolpath unchanged.

Expected: the path is regenerated with the new stepover (fewer, wider passes), or the value is refused by name if it is not allowed for the tool (for example larger than the tool diameter). A value that is silently ignored or clamped is wrong either way: the user believes the program cuts with a load it does not cut with.

Not yet known (the report names no operation): which operation and tool. To find out first:
- which operations read `stepover` at generate, and whether any takes it from somewhere else (a tool default, optimal load, a cached value) and ignores the edited field;
- whether the edit reaches the operation (the `EditOperationParams` command writes it) and marks the path dirty so it regenerates;
- whether a clamp (to the tool diameter or a fraction of it) makes 3 mm and 25 mm the same value without telling the user;
- the trochoidal slot, plain Slot and Adaptive, which the maintainer was testing today (issues 1503, 1504), are the first suspects.

Done means: for every operation that shows a stepover field, a test changes the stepover and asserts that the generated path changes (pass spacing follows the value) or that the value is refused by name; a clamp, where one is right, is shown in the operation's message; an operation that has no use for stepover does not show the field.
